<PaperLayout
  title="Attention Is All You Need"
  eyebrow="NLP · Transformer · 2017 / NeurIPS"
  authors="Ashish Vaswani · Noam Shazeer · Niki Parmar · Jakob Uszkoreit · Llion Jones · Aidan N. Gomez · Łukasz Kaiser · Illia Polosukhin"
  arxivUrl="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762"
  subtitle="A structured reading of the paper that moved attention from a helpful add-on to the main computational engine of modern language models."
  sections={sections}
  annotations={annotations}
  initialAnnotation="attention"
  compactTitle={true}
>
        <section id="overview" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Overview</h2>

          <div class="guide-box">
            <strong>Reading guide</strong>
            <p>
              This paper is best understood as a turning point in how sequence models move information.
              Earlier models processed a sentence through recurrence or local convolution; the Transformer
              instead lets each token directly look up relevant information from the rest of the sequence.
              The central question is not simply “what modules does it use?”, but “how does information
              travel between words?”
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            <em>Attention Is All You Need</em> introduces the
            <button class="anno" data-anno="transformer">Transformer</button>,
            a model for
            <button class="anno" data-anno="sequenceTransduction">sequence transduction</button>
            that removes recurrence and convolution from the main architecture. The paper evaluates this
            idea on machine translation, but its impact reaches far beyond translation: the same architectural
            principle later becomes the foundation of BERT, GPT-style models, many vision models, and the
            modern large language model ecosystem.
          </p>

          <p>
            The title is intentionally bold, but it should be read carefully. The Transformer is not made of
            attention alone. It still relies on embeddings, positional encodings, feed-forward networks,
            residual connections, layer normalization, dropout, and a final softmax layer. What the title
            really argues is more specific: for modeling relationships between positions in a sequence,
            attention can replace the recurrent and convolutional machinery that dominated earlier neural
            sequence models.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow history-flow">
            <span>RNN / LSTM / GRU</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>CNN sequence models</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Attention as helper</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Attention as architecture</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="story" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Why this paper mattered</h2>

          <p>
            Before the Transformer, strong translation systems usually depended on
            <button class="anno" data-anno="rnn">RNNs</button>,
            especially <button class="anno" data-anno="lstm">LSTMs</button> and
            <button class="anno" data-anno="gru">GRUs</button>. These models had a natural way to process order:
            read token 1, then token 2, then token 3. That was elegant, and it matched our intuition about language as
            a sequence. But it also created a computational bottleneck. The hidden state at position <code>t</code>
            could not be fully computed until position <code>t−1</code> was done, which made long sequences harder to
            parallelize during training.
          </p>

          <p>
            There was already a counter-movement. Models such as
            <button class="anno" data-anno="extendedNeuralGpu">Extended Neural GPU</button>,
            <button class="anno" data-anno="bytenet">ByteNet</button>, and
            <button class="anno" data-anno="convs2s">ConvS2S</button> tried to reduce sequential computation with
            <button class="anno" data-anno="cnn">convolutional sequence models</button>. They replaced the step-by-step
            recurrence of RNNs with convolutional computation that could be applied to many positions at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            The key difference was how far information could travel in one layer.
            <button class="anno" data-anno="convs2s">ConvS2S</button> used stacked convolutions: fast and parallel, but still
            local, so distant words needed multiple layers to influence each other.
            <button class="anno" data-anno="bytenet">ByteNet</button> used dilated convolutions to expand the receptive field
            more quickly, reducing the path between faraway positions to a logarithmic scale.
            <button class="anno" data-anno="extendedNeuralGpu">Extended Neural GPU</button> belonged to a related line of work
            that treated sequence processing more like repeated updates over a parallel memory-like grid. These models were
            important because they showed that sequence modeling did not have to be strictly recurrent. But they still did
            not make every token globally visible to every other token in a single step.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Transformer asks a more radical question: if attention already lets a decoder look directly
            at any source position, why not let every token look directly at every other token? That is the
            step from attention as an alignment tool to
            <button class="anno" data-anno="selfAttention">self-attention</button> as the core architecture.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">Historical aside</span>
            <p>
              There is an interesting twist in the story. The Transformer came from Google, and Google quickly used
              Transformer-based models in major systems, including BERT for Search. Yet the first Transformer-based product
              that many people experienced as a cultural event was ChatGPT, released by OpenAI. That did not happen simply
              because OpenAI had a different architecture. GPT was also a Transformer. The difference was in how the model
              was scaled, trained as a decoder-only language model, adapted to follow instructions, aligned with human
              feedback, and finally presented through an extremely simple chat interface. The architecture made the product
              possible, but product timing, deployment choices, risk tolerance, and alignment work shaped who brought that
              experience to the public first.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="problem" class="reading-section">
          <h2>The bottleneck it attacked</h2>

          <p>
            The central enemy is not simply that “RNNs are old.” The real problem is
            <strong>sequential dependence</strong>. In an RNN, information moves through a chain:
            the representation at one position depends on the representation before it. If token 1 needs
            to influence token 30, the signal must pass through many intermediate hidden states. This makes
            long-distance dependencies harder to learn and prevents the model from fully parallelizing
            computation across positions.
          </p>

          <div class="comparison-grid">
            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>RNN information path</strong>
              <p>
                <code>x₁ → h₁ → h₂ → h₃ → ... → hₙ</code>. The order is natural, but the path is long,
                and training has an unavoidable left-to-right dependency.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>CNN information path</strong>
              <p>
                Convolutions can process positions in parallel, but each layer sees only a local window.
                Distant tokens meet only after enough layers expand the receptive field.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Self-attention path</strong>
              <p>
                Any token can attend to any other token in one layer. The path length between two positions
                is constant, no matter how far apart they are.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Transformer trade-off</strong>
              <p>
                Full self-attention costs <code>O(n²)</code> in sequence length because every token is compared
                with every other token. For sentence-level translation, <code>n</code> is usually manageable.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="architecture" class="reading-section">
          <h2>The architecture as a whole</h2>

          <p>
            The Transformer keeps the familiar encoder-decoder shape of earlier translation systems. The
            <button class="anno" data-anno="encoder">encoder</button> reads the source sentence and builds
            contextual representations for every source token. The
            <button class="anno" data-anno="decoder">decoder</button> generates the target sentence one token
            at a time. What changes is not the high-level translation workflow, but the internal mechanism
            used inside each block.
          </p>

          <div class="architecture-flow">
            <span>Token embedding</span>
            <span>+</span>
            <span>Positional encoding</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Encoder stack × 6</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Decoder stack × 6</span>
            <span>→</span>
            <span>Linear + softmax</span>
          </div>

          <p>
            “Six layers” means six blocks with the same structure, not one block reused with shared weights.
            Each layer has its own parameters. The encoder stack repeats one kind of block six times; the
            decoder stack repeats a slightly larger block six times. This stacked design lets the model refine
            token representations gradually, from surface-level word information toward more task-specific
            contextual information.
          </p>

          <div class="comparison-grid stack-grid">
            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>One encoder layer</strong>
              <p>
                <code>Self-attention → Add &amp; Norm → FFN → Add &amp; Norm</code>
              </p>
              <p>
                The encoder layer lets every source token gather information from the full source sentence,
                then uses a
                <button class="anno" data-anno="ffn">position-wise feed-forward network</button>
                to refine each token representation independently.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>One decoder layer</strong>
              <p>
                <code>Masked self-attention → Add &amp; Norm → Cross-attention → Add &amp; Norm → FFN → Add &amp; Norm</code>
              </p>
              <p>
                The decoder first looks only at previously generated target tokens, then uses
                <button class="anno" data-anno="crossAttention">encoder-decoder attention</button>
                to query the encoder output and decide which source positions matter for the next prediction.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-grid">
            {architectureSteps.map((step) => (
              <div class="comparison-card">
                <strong>{step.title}</strong>
                <p>{step.text}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <p>
            Around each sublayer, the model applies a
            <button class="anno" data-anno="residual">residual connection</button> followed by
            <button class="anno" data-anno="layernorm">layer normalization</button>. This is written as
            <code>LayerNorm(x + Sublayer(x))</code>. These details are not decorative: residual connections
            preserve information and improve gradient flow, while layer normalization keeps the activations
            stable as the model becomes deeper.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-figure attention-figure">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 900 360" role="img" aria-label="Simplified Transformer encoder-decoder flow">
              <defs>
                <marker id="arrowhead" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="6" refX="7" refY="3" orient="auto">
                  <polygon points="0 0, 8 3, 0 6" class="arrow-head"></polygon>
                </marker>
              </defs>

              <text x="170" y="34" class="section-title">Encoder</text>
              <text x="650" y="34" class="section-title">Decoder</text>

              <rect x="60" y="70" width="230" height="55" rx="12"></rect>
              <text x="175" y="98">Input embeddings + PE</text>

              <rect x="60" y="155" width="230" height="55" rx="12"></rect>
              <text x="175" y="183">Multi-head self-attention</text>

              <rect x="60" y="240" width="230" height="55" rx="12"></rect>
              <text x="175" y="268">Position-wise FFN</text>

              <rect x="520" y="70" width="250" height="55" rx="12"></rect>
              <text x="645" y="98">Output embeddings + PE</text>

              <rect x="520" y="155" width="250" height="55" rx="12"></rect>
              <text x="645" y="183">Masked self-attention</text>

              <rect x="520" y="240" width="250" height="55" rx="12"></rect>
              <text x="645" y="268">Cross-attention + FFN</text>

              <g class="arrows">
                <line x1="175" y1="125" x2="175" y2="150"></line>
                <line x1="175" y1="210" x2="175" y2="235"></line>
