                <line x1="645" y1="210" x2="645" y2="235"></line>
                <line x1="292" y1="267" x2="514" y2="267"></line>
              </g>

              <text x="405" y="245" class="axis-label">encoder outputs become K,V for cross-attention</text>
            </svg>

            <figcaption>
              A simplified view: the encoder contextualizes the source sequence; the decoder generates
              causally and queries the encoder output.
            </figcaption>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="attention" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Attention, QKV, and square-root scaling</h2>

          <p>
            The most useful way to understand
            <button class="anno" data-anno="attention">attention</button> is as soft retrieval. A token does not passively
            receive a hidden state from its neighbor. It asks a question, compares that question to candidate keys,
            and retrieves a weighted mixture of values.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Symbol</th>
                  <th>Name</th>
                  <th>Interpretation</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {qkvRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>{row.symbol}</code></td>
                    <td>{row.name}</td>
                    <td>{row.meaning}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The important point is that <button class="anno" data-anno="qkv">Q, K, and V</button> are learned projections.
            Given token representations <code>X</code>, the model forms <code>Q = XW<sup>Q</sup></code>,
            <code>K = XW<sup>K</sup></code>, and <code>V = XW<sup>V</sup></code>. The same token can therefore
            play three roles: it can ask for information, advertise itself to others, and provide content when selected.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK<sup>T</sup> / √d<sub>k</sub>)V</code>
          </div>

          <p>
            The term <code>QK<sup>T</sup></code> creates an <code>n × n</code> matrix of compatibility scores.
            Row <code>i</code>, column <code>j</code> means: how much should token <code>i</code> attend to token
            <code>j</code>? <button class="anno" data-anno="softmax">Softmax</button> turns each row into attention
            weights, and multiplying by <code>V</code> creates weighted averages of value vectors.
          </p>

          <h3>Why divide by √d<sub>k</sub>?</h3>

          <p>
            Suppose the components of <code>q</code> and <code>k</code> are independent, with mean <code>0</code>
            and variance <code>1</code>. Their dot product is:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card">
            <code>q · k = Σ<sub>i=1</sub><sup>d<sub>k</sub></sup> q<sub>i</sub>k<sub>i</sub></code>
          </div>

          <p>
            Since there are <code>d<sub>k</sub></code> terms, the variance of the sum grows roughly like
            <code>d<sub>k</sub></code>. In other words, larger key/query dimensions tend to produce larger and more
            extreme dot-product scores.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem shows up inside <button class="anno" data-anno="softmax">softmax</button>. If the scores are
            moderately sized, softmax still leaves room for several positions to matter. For example:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card">
            <code>softmax([1, 2, 3]) ≈ [0.09, 0.24, 0.67]</code>
          </div>

          <p>
            But if the same pattern is scaled up, the distribution becomes much sharper:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card">
            <code>softmax([10, 20, 30]) ≈ [0.00, 0.00, 1.00]</code>
          </div>

          <p>
            Now one position receives almost all the probability, while gradients for the other positions become tiny.
            Dividing by <code>√d<sub>k</sub></code> pulls the standard deviation of the dot products back toward a stable
            scale, keeping softmax from becoming too saturated. This is the “scaled” part of scaled dot-product attention.
          </p>

          <h3>Dot-product attention vs. additive attention</h3>

          <p>
            Dot-product attention scores compatibility with a simple vector dot product:
            <code>score(q, k) = q · k</code>. In matrix form, this becomes <code>QK<sup>T</sup></code>, which is why
            it is so efficient: all query-key comparisons can be computed as one large, highly optimized matrix
            multiplication.
          </p>

          <p>
            <button class="anno" data-anno="additive">Additive attention</button>, associated with Bahdanau attention,
            uses a learned scoring network instead of a direct dot product. A common way to write it is
            <code>score(q, k) = v<sup>T</sup> tanh(W<sub>q</sub>q + W<sub>k</sub>k)</code>. In words, the query and key
            are first transformed, combined, passed through a nonlinearity, and then scored by another learned vector.
          </p>

          <p>
            Additive attention can be expressive, and it was very important in earlier neural machine translation.
            The Transformer uses dot-product attention because it is much faster and more memory-efficient in practice,
            especially when implemented with batched matrix multiplication. The scaling factor <code>√d<sub>k</sub></code>
            helps dot-product attention avoid the large-score problem that appears when the key/query dimension grows.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="multihead" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Multi-head attention</h2>

          <p>
            A single attention distribution gives the model one way to look at the sentence. But language
            contains many relationships at once: pronouns, modifiers, syntax, local context, long-range
            dependencies, and translation alignment. The
            <button class="anno" data-anno="multihead">multi-head</button> design lets the model learn several
            views in parallel.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              MultiHead(Q, K, V) = Concat(head<sub>1</sub>, ..., head<sub>h</sub>)W<sup>O</sup>
            </code>
            <br />
            <code>
              head<sub>i</sub> = Attention(QW<sub>i</sub><sup>Q</sup>, KW<sub>i</sub><sup>K</sup>, VW<sub>i</sub><sup>V</sup>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Symbol</th>
                  <th>Meaning</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {multiHeadRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td><code set:html={row.symbol}></code></td>
                    <td set:html={row.meaning}></td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            In the base model, <code>d<sub>model</sub> = 512</code>, <code>h = 8</code>, and each head uses
            <code>d<sub>k</sub> = d<sub>v</sub> = 64</code>. Eight heads of 64 dimensions concatenate back to
            512 dimensions. This keeps the total cost similar to one full-width head while giving the model
            multiple learned subspaces.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">How to read the formula</span>
            <p>
              Each head receives its own projected <code>Q</code>, <code>K</code>, and <code>V</code>.
              It performs scaled dot-product attention independently. The heads are concatenated, then
              <code>W<sup>O</sup></code> mixes them back into the model's main representation space.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="position" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Positional encoding</h2>

          <p>
            Self-attention by itself is permutation-friendly: without extra information, the model can see that a set of
            tokens exists, but not their order. That is why the model adds
            <button class="anno" data-anno="positionalEncoding">positional encoding</button> to token embeddings.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              PE(pos, 2i) = sin(pos / 10000<sup>2i / d<sub>model</sub></sup>)
            </code>
            <br />
            <code>
              PE(pos, 2i + 1) = cos(pos / 10000<sup>2i / d<sub>model</sub></sup>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            Each position gets a <code>d<sub>model</sub></code>-dimensional vector. Even dimensions use sine;
            odd dimensions use cosine. The frequencies differ by dimension. A single sine curve does oscillate
            between −1 and 1, but the whole position vector is a multi-frequency fingerprint. One dimension
            alone is not the point; the combination is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why not just use position numbers like 1, 2, 3? A raw number grows without bound and gives the
            network a poor representation of local and relative distance. Sinusoidal encodings stay numerically
            stable and create structured relationships between positions.
          </p>

          <h3>What “relative position is easier to learn” means</h3>

          <p>
            Many language patterns depend on offsets: previous word, next word, three tokens away, far away.
            The paper's sinusoidal choice makes a fixed offset <code>k</code> mathematically regular. Through
            trigonometric identities, <code>PE(pos + k)</code> can be represented as a linear function of
            <code>PE(pos)</code> for a fixed <code>k</code>.
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card">
            <code>sin(pos + k) = sin(pos)cos(k) + cos(pos)sin(k)</code>
            <br />
            <code>cos(pos + k) = cos(pos)cos(k) − sin(pos)sin(k)</code>
          </div>

          <p>
            Here, <code>pos</code> plays the role of the current position, and <code>k</code> is the offset.
            More precisely, each sinusoidal dimension uses a scaled position such as
            <code>pos / wavelength</code>, so the actual expression is closer to
            <code>sin((pos + k) / wavelength)</code>. But the same idea still holds: for a fixed offset
            <code>k</code>, the representation at <code>pos + k</code> can be written as a predictable
            combination of the sine and cosine values at <code>pos</code> under the same frequency.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is why sinusoidal encoding is useful for relative distance. The model is not merely given
            isolated labels like “position 7” and “position 10.” It receives position vectors whose relationships
            contain regular patterns. That makes it easier for attention layers to learn ideas like “look one token
            back,” “look three tokens ahead,” or “prefer nearby tokens,” even though those rules are not hard-coded.
          </p>

          <p>
            This does not mean the model is handed an alignment table. It means the encoding gives the model
            a convenient structure for learning “distance-like” relationships. In translation, English and French
            words often move to different positions. Positional encoding only tells each side its own internal
            order; the actual cross-language correspondence is learned by encoder-decoder attention.
          </p>

          <div class="comparison-grid">
            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>English source</strong>
              <p><code>"the red car"</code>: <code>"red"</code> is before <code>"car"</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>French target</strong>
              <p><code>"la voiture rouge"</code>: <code>"rouge"</code> is after <code>"voiture"</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Position encoding does</strong>
              <p>Represent order inside each sequence.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Cross-attention does</strong>
              <p>Learn that <code>"rouge"</code> should retrieve information from <code>"red"</code>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="why" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Why self-attention</h2>

          <p>
            The case for self-attention rests on three questions. How much computation does one layer need?
            How many steps must be executed sequentially? And how short is the path between two distant
            positions? A good sequence layer should be parallelizable and should let faraway tokens exchange
            information through short paths.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Layer type</th>
                  <th>Complexity</th>
                  <th>Sequential ops</th>
                  <th>Path length</th>
                  <th>Why it matters</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {complexityRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.layer}</td>
                    <td><code set:html={row.complexity}></code></td>
                    <td><code set:html={row.sequential}></code></td>
                    <td><code set:html={row.path}></code></td>
                    <td set:html={row.reason}></td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3>Where the complexity numbers come from</h3>

          <div class="comparison-grid">
            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Self-attention: O(n²d)</strong>
              <p>
                With <code>n</code> tokens, every token compares itself with every other token. That creates
                <code>n × n</code> query-key scores. Each score is a dot product over roughly <code>d</code>
                dimensions, so the attention score computation is <code>O(n²d)</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                The sequential depth is <code>O(1)</code> because all pairwise comparisons can be computed in
                parallel as matrix multiplication. The maximum path length is also <code>O(1)</code>: token
                <code>i</code> can attend directly to token <code>j</code> in one layer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Restricted self-attention: O(rnd)</strong>
              <p>
                Restricted self-attention limits each token to a local neighborhood of size <code>r</code> instead
                of attending to all <code>n</code> positions. That means there are only <code>n × r</code>
                query-key comparisons rather than <code>n²</code>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Each comparison still costs about <code>O(d)</code>, so the total is <code>O(rnd)</code>. It remains
                parallel across positions, but long-distance information now has to hop across neighborhoods.
                The maximum path length becomes roughly <code>O(n / r)</code>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Recurrent layer: O(nd²)</strong>
              <p>
                A recurrent layer updates one hidden state per token. Each update usually applies a learned
                transformation from a <code>d</code>-dimensional hidden state to another <code>d</code>-dimensional
                hidden state, which costs about <code>O(d²)</code> per position.
              </p>
              <p>
                Across <code>n</code> positions, that gives <code>O(nd²)</code>. The sequential depth is
                <code>O(n)</code> because state <code>h<sub>t</sub></code> depends on <code>h<sub>t−1</sub></code>.
                The path from the first token to the last token also has length <code>O(n)</code>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-card">
              <strong>Convolutional layer: O(knd²)</strong>
              <p>
                A convolutional sequence layer applies a kernel of width <code>k</code> at every one of the
                <code>n</code> positions. At each position, it combines <code>k</code> neighboring vectors and
                maps them through learned transformations over <code>d</code>-dimensional features.
              </p>
              <p>
                This gives roughly <code>O(knd²)</code>. The layer is parallel across positions, so its sequential
                depth is <code>O(1)</code>. But a single layer only sees a local window. To connect distant tokens,
                information must move through multiple convolutional layers. With receptive field expansion, the
                maximum path length is written as <code>O(log<sub>k</sub> n)</code>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3>When is self-attention cheaper than recurrence?</h3>

          <p>
            The key comparison is self-attention versus recurrence. Self-attention costs
            <code>O(n²d)</code>; recurrence costs <code>O(nd²)</code>. Ignoring constant factors, self-attention
            is cheaper when:
          </p>

          <div class="formula-card">
            <code>n²d &lt; nd² &nbsp;⇒&nbsp; n &lt; d</code>
          </div>

          <p>
            In sentence-level translation, the sequence length <code>n</code> is often much smaller than the
            representation dimension <code>d</code>, such as <code>d<sub>model</sub> = 512</code>. This is why full
            self-attention can be attractive despite the quadratic term in sequence length.
          </p>

          <p>
            The deeper advantage is not just raw complexity. Self-attention gives every pair of positions a
            direct route. That short path is especially useful for long-distance dependencies: a word near the
            beginning of a sentence can influence a word near the end without passing through a long chain of
            recurrent states or many layers of local convolution.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="training" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Training recipe</h2>

          <p>
            The paper's result is not architecture alone. It also depends on a careful training setup: subword tokenization,
            large batches, Adam, learning-rate warmup, dropout, label smoothing, checkpoint averaging, and beam search.
          </p>

          <div class="results-table-wrap">
            <table class="results-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Item</th>
                  <th>Configuration</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {trainingConfig.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.item}</td>
                    <td set:html={row.value}></td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3>Learning rate schedule</h3>

          <div class="formula-card emphasis-formula">
            <code>
              lrate = d<sub>model</sub><sup>−0.5</sup> · min(step_num<sup>−0.5</sup>, step_num · warmup_steps<sup>−1.5</sup>)
            </code>
          </div>

          <p>
            The model uses <button class="anno" data-anno="adam">Adam</button> plus
            <button class="anno" data-anno="warmup">warmup</button>. Early learning rate increases linearly for
            <code>4000</code> steps, then decays roughly like the inverse square root of the step number. Warmup prevents
            unstable early jumps; later decay makes updates more precise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <button class="anno" data-anno="dropout">Dropout</button> is applied after sublayer outputs and after the sum
            of embeddings and positional encodings. <button class="anno" data-anno="labelSmoothing">Label smoothing</button>
            makes targets less overconfident. The paper notes a subtle trade-off: label smoothing can hurt perplexity because
            the model is less certain, while improving accuracy and BLEU.
          </p>

          <p>
            For evaluation, the authors use <button class="anno" data-anno="checkpointAveraging">checkpoint averaging</button>:
            base models average the last <code>5</code> checkpoints, and big models average the last <code>20</code>. They decode with
            <button class="anno" data-anno="beamSearch">beam search</button> using beam size <code>4</code> and length penalty
            <code>0.6</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="results" class="reading-section">
          <h2>Results and comparisons</h2>

          <p>
            The headline result is simple: the Transformer is both strong and efficient. On WMT 2014
            English-German, Transformer big reaches <code>28.4 BLEU</code>, more than <code>2 BLEU</code>
            above the previous best reported systems, including ensembles. On English-French, it reaches
            <code>41.8 BLEU</code> as a single model.
          </p>

          <p>
            The important comparison is not only “which model has the highest BLEU?” but also “how much
            computation did it take to get there?” Several earlier systems achieved strong translation quality
            by using recurrent models, convolutional models, mixture-of-experts layers, reinforcement-learning
            refinements, or ensembles. The Transformer competes with all of them while using substantially
            less training cost than many previous high-performing systems.
          </p>

