          <h3>What the baselines are doing</h3>

          <p>
            The baselines are not random older models; they represent the strongest competing directions in
            neural machine translation at the time. Some are recurrent encoder-decoder systems with attention,
            some are convolutional sequence models, some use very large sparse computation, and some combine
            multiple models into an ensemble. This matters because the Transformer is not being compared only
            against weak RNNs. It is being compared against several serious attempts to make translation models
            more accurate or more efficient.
          </p>

          <div class="concept-grid">
            {modelGlossary.map((item) => (
              <div class="concept-card">
                <h3>{item.name}</h3>
                <p>{item.text}</p>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          <p>
            Read these baselines as a map of the field before the Transformer. <strong>GNMT</strong> represents
            the mature recurrent translation pipeline: deep LSTMs, attention, and production-oriented engineering.
            <strong>ConvS2S</strong> and <strong>ByteNet</strong> represent the convolutional push toward better
            parallelism. <strong>Deep-Att + PosUnk</strong> represents an attention-heavy recurrent translation
            line with explicit handling of rare or unknown words. <strong>MoE</strong> explores scaling model
            capacity through sparsely activated expert networks. The ensemble rows show what happens when
            several trained models are combined at inference time, usually improving quality but increasing
            cost and complexity.
          </p>

          <h3>What the model variation experiments teach</h3>

          <p>
            The model variation results tell the architectural story behind the headline score. A single
            attention head is worse than multi-head attention, which supports the idea that different heads
            learn different relational views of the sentence. But too many heads can also hurt, because if
            the total model dimension is fixed, each head becomes too narrow to represent rich query-key
            compatibility.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reducing the key/query dimension hurts quality, suggesting that deciding whether a query and a
            key match is not a trivial operation. Larger models generally perform better, dropout is important
            for preventing overfitting, and learned positional embeddings perform almost the same as sinusoidal
            encodings on the tested translation task. In other words, the paper’s best result is supported by
            a set of design choices rather than by one isolated trick.
          </p>

          <div class="paper-passage">
            <span class="passage-label">How to interpret the result</span>
            <p>
              The paper does not merely report “higher BLEU.” It connects the gain to the earlier architectural
              argument: shorter paths for long-distance dependency, fewer sequential operations, and better GPU
              parallelization. The result is persuasive because the same design that explains the speed advantage
              also delivers state-of-the-art translation quality.
            </p>
          </div>
