                {timelineRows.map((row) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{row.year}</td>
                    <td>{row.event}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Why was it called “Transformer”? At the paper level, the name fits the sequence-transduction framing:
            the model transforms one sequence into another. It also names the architectural transformation happening
            inside the model: representations are repeatedly transformed by attention and feed-forward blocks rather than
            updated by recurrence. The name became broader than the original machine-translation setting because the same
            block turned out to scale across many modalities and tasks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Google-versus-OpenAI question is best answered carefully. Google created the architecture and later used
            Transformer-based models in major products. OpenAI, however, pushed the decoder-only generative line into a
            public conversational interface. Architecture was necessary, but not sufficient; product timing and alignment
            strategy mattered too.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="takeaways" class="reading-section final-takeaways">
          <h2>Takeaways</h2>

          <ol>
            <li>The Transformer keeps encoder-decoder translation but replaces recurrence/convolution with attention-based token communication.</li>
            <li>Attention is soft retrieval: Q asks, K matches, V supplies information.</li>
            <li>Square-root scaling keeps dot-product scores from making softmax too sharp.</li>
            <li>Multi-head attention gives the model multiple learned views of the same sequence.</li>
            <li>Sinusoidal positional encoding gives order information and a convenient structure for relative offsets.</li>
            <li>The paper's empirical claim is not only better BLEU, but better BLEU with far more parallelizable training.</li>
            <li>The modern LLM era begins here architecturally, but ChatGPT required later choices: decoder-only pretraining, scaling, instruction tuning, feedback, and product design.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>
      
